					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/monteparnas" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2926286); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2926286&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606101119" alt="Monteparnas" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2926286)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/monteparnas" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2926286); return false;" >Monteparnas</a></span> <span class="odate time_1520593967 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Mar 2018 11:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3733496">
									

<p>I actually disliked it, though I dig part of the premise.</p>
<p>First, it is clearly Safe. The impulse of people to know their fate is completely mundane and do not come from the SCP in any meaningful way. It actually never broke containment, people broke protocol, and even this in a reasonably minor way for the Foundation.</p>
<p>Second, the effort to keep it away from people hugely outweigh the benefit. Seriously, the Foundation can't even say for sure that this thing creates the outcome, not just predicts it. Some of the predictions put this death far in the future.</p>
<p>Given the kind of thing those people work with, if one can't deal with her own mortality, she isn't cut for the Foundation anyway.</p>
<p>I like the idea of working the psychology of people dealing with this kind of knowledge, and this part of the article is well delivered, as much as the experiments done. But the rest of the article is too much afraid of too little danger. And most of the experiments denied could have been done, by the way.</p>

							</div>

								
					
